# cPanel-managed Apache reverse proxy for the AAU platform.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THIS IS A REFERENCE, NOT A FILE TO COPY OVER httpd.conf
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The audited host runs cPanel/WHM with Apache 2.4.68 owning ports 80 and 443.
# cPanel GENERATES /etc/apache2/conf/httpd.conf from its own templates and
# rebuilds it on account changes, SSL renewals, EasyApache updates and more.
# Anything written directly into that file is lost, silently, at the worst
# possible moment.
#
# The supported integration surface is cPanel's per-vhost include directories.
# Place this content in the include that matches the account and vhost, then
# have cPanel rebuild and restart:
#
#   # SSL (443) vhost include for one user's one domain:
#   /etc/apache2/conf.d/userdata/ssl/2_4/<cpanel-user>/<domain>/aau-proxy.conf
#   # plain HTTP (80) equivalent, if anything is needed there at all:
#   /etc/apache2/conf.d/userdata/std/2_4/<cpanel-user>/<domain>/aau-proxy.conf
#
#   /usr/local/cpanel/scripts/ensure_vhost_includes --user=<cpanel-user>
#   /scripts/rebuildhttpdconf && /scripts/restartsrv_httpd
#
# Confirm the exact paths against the cPanel version on the host before
# writing anything: they are version-specific, and this repository cannot
# verify them. PENDING REAL VPS.
#
# ── Scope: this must capture ONE vhost, not the server ────────────────────
# The host also serves mail and cPanel's own interfaces. A proxy rule placed at
# server scope, or a `ProxyPass /` in a shared include, would capture:
#
#   mail.<domain>        webmail.<domain>      cpanel.<domain>
#   whm.<domain>         autodiscover.<domain> autoconfig.<domain>
#   cpcalendars.<domain> cpcontacts.<domain>
#   /cpanel /webmail /whm  proxy subdomains and their alias paths
#   /.well-known/acme-challenge  (AutoSSL renewal — breaking this breaks TLS)
#
# Breaking any of those breaks mail, webmail, or certificate renewal for the
# whole account. Using a per-user, per-domain userdata include is what keeps
# the rule scoped to exactly one vhost, and the exclusions below are defence in
# depth on top of that.
#
# ── Routing model ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   public HTTPS name -> CSF -> cPanel Apache :443 -> this proxy -> 127.0.0.1
#
#   production  ->  127.0.0.1:3000
#   staging     ->  127.0.0.1:13000
#
# The application ports are bound to loopback only and are never publicly
# reachable. That is a Docker binding, not a firewall: CSF must still deny
# 3000/3001/13000/13001 from the outside, and that has to be verified ON THE
# HOST. PENDING REAL VPS.

# ── Never proxy these ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ACME first: if AutoSSL cannot answer a challenge, the certificate expires and
# the site goes down in a way that looks like an application failure.
ProxyPass        /.well-known/acme-challenge  !
ProxyPass        /cpanel                      !
ProxyPass        /webmail                     !
ProxyPass        /whm                         !
ProxyPass        /cpcalendars                 !
ProxyPass        /cpcontacts                  !
ProxyPass        /autodiscover                !
ProxyPass        /autoconfig                  !

# ── The application ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `nocanon` passes the raw, un-normalised path through. Payload's admin and
# REST routes carry encoded characters in IDs and query values, and Apache's
# canonicalisation would rewrite them.
ProxyPreserveHost On
ProxyPass        /  http://127.0.0.1:3000/  nocanon retry=0 timeout=120 connectiontimeout=5
ProxyPassReverse /  http://127.0.0.1:3000/

# ── Forwarding headers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The application resolves its own public origin from the origin baked into the
# image at build time, so it does not DEPEND on these — but they must still be
# correct, or client IPs in logs and rate limiting are wrong, and anything that
# inspects the scheme sees plain HTTP.
#
# X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Host are set by mod_proxy_http itself.
RequestHeader set X-Forwarded-Proto "https"
RequestHeader set X-Forwarded-Port  "443"

# ── Request-body handling to the backend ─────────────────────────────────
# CAREFUL: these mod_proxy variables are PRESENCE-activated, not value-
# activated. The Apache documentation is explicit — "environment variables that
# don't specify specific values are enabled when set to any value" — so
# `SetEnv proxy-nokeepalive 0` does NOT disable it. It ENABLES it, closing the
# backend connection after every request, which is the opposite of what a "0"
# reads as. That line used to be here and has been removed rather than
# corrected: keepalive to the backend is the Apache default and needs no
# directive at all.
#
# `proxy-sendchunked` governs how the REQUEST BODY is forwarded upstream: it
# lets a chunked request be streamed to the backend instead of being spooled to
# disk to compute a Content-Length. That matters for large admin media uploads.
# It has nothing to do with how RESPONSES are streamed to the client, which is
# what an earlier comment here claimed — Next.js RSC streaming is unaffected by
# it either way.
SetEnv proxy-sendchunked 1

# ── Timeouts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `timeout=120` above covers a slow first render or a large upload. It must be
# LONGER than the application's own request handling and SHORTER than any
# client-facing idle timeout, or a request that is still being served is cut off
# by the proxy and reported as a 502 that the application logs know nothing
# about.

# ── Upload size ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Payload accepts media uploads. Apache's LimitRequestBody defaults vary by
# cPanel configuration; set it deliberately rather than discovering the limit
# from a user's failed upload. 64 MiB, matching the nginx reference.
LimitRequestBody 67108864

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  STAGING
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Identical, in the staging domain's own userdata include, pointing at 13000:
#
#   ProxyPass        /  http://127.0.0.1:13000/  nocanon retry=0 timeout=120
#   ProxyPassReverse /  http://127.0.0.1:13000/
#
# Staging must have its OWN hostname. The public origin is baked into the image
# and drives CORS, CSRF and cookie scope, so serving staging from a production
# hostname would issue cookies for the production domain.
#
# ── Internal testing before DNS and TLS exist ─────────────────────────────
# The operator can reach staging on 127.0.0.1:13000 over an SSH tunnel before
# any of this is wired. That is an INFRASTRUCTURE smoke test — container health,
# database, Redis, migrations, persistence — and it is not equivalent to real
# staging.
#
# It cannot exercise the HTTPS origin, `Secure` cookies, CSRF, admin login,
# streaming or uploads through the proxy, because those depend on a real HTTPS
# origin. Do NOT relax the production origin rules to make an HTTP tunnel behave
# like public staging: that would weaken the exact control the tunnel cannot
# test. See deploy/STAGING_VPS_CONTRACT.md, "Two distinct test phases".
